    seoTitle: "AI in Marketing for Kenyan Businesses",
    metaDescription:
      "What AI can and can't do for marketing in Kenya right now, where it saves real time, and how a small business can start using it without wasting money.",
    date: "March 2025",
    readMinutes: 12,
    excerpt: "The AI hype cycle has peaked, and what's left is something more useful: a realistic picture of what AI can actually do for marketing right now, what it can't do, and where the real…",
    ctaTitle: "Ready to put AI into your marketing?",
    ctaBody: "We design and build practical AI marketing systems for East African businesses.",
    image: "/brand/blog/ai-in-marketing.jpg",
    body: `<p>The AI hype cycle has peaked, and what's left is something more useful: a realistic picture of what AI can actually do for marketing right now, what it can't do, and where the real opportunities are for businesses operating in the Kenyan market.</p><h2>What AI Is Actually Good At in Marketing</h2><ul><li><strong>Content at scale:</strong> AI produces first drafts of social posts, email sequences, ad copy variations, and article outlines in a fraction of the time it takes a human writer. The velocity gain is real and measurable.</li><li><strong>Personalisation at scale:</strong> AI enables truly personalised email and ad content based on user behaviour — the kind of 1-to-1 marketing previously only available to the largest brands.</li><li><strong>Data analysis and insight:</strong> AI can surface patterns in your marketing data that a human analyst would take weeks to find — and it never gets tired or distracted.</li><li><strong>Chatbots and customer service:</strong> Well-built AI chatbots handle the 80% of customer queries that are repetitive and predictable — freeing your human team for the 20% that require real judgment.</li></ul><h2>What AI Cannot Do</h2><p>AI cannot understand your customer's cultural context the way a human who grew up in that culture can. It cannot build genuine relationships. It cannot have a genuinely controversial opinion that creates real brand differentiation. And it cannot create the kind of unexpected, emotionally resonant creative that makes people stop, feel something real, and tell their friends.</p><blockquote><p>AI is the most powerful amplifier in the history of marketing. But amplifiers don't have a sound of their own. Your brand still needs one.</p></blockquote><h2>The Kenyan Opportunity</h2><p>For Kenyan businesses, the AI opportunity is particularly significant because the adoption gap between large and small businesses is narrowing faster here than in markets with entrenched legacy systems. A Nairobi startup with the right AI tools can execute marketing programmes that would have required a team of 20 people three years ago.</p><h2>Where to Start</h2><p>Start with your highest-cost, most repetitive tasks. If your team spends 40% of its time writing first drafts of content, start there. If you're losing leads because no one responds to WhatsApp enquiries quickly enough, start with a chatbot. Pick one problem, solve it with AI, measure the impact, then expand from there. The businesses that get the best results from AI treat it as a tool to be mastered, not a solution to be deployed and forgotten.</p>`,
