    title: "SEO for African Businesses: Why the Opportunity Is Bigger Than You Think",
    seoTitle: "SEO for Kenyan & African Businesses",
    metaDescription:
      "In most Kenyan industries, competition for the top Google spots is still low. Why SEO is a big opportunity for African businesses and where to start.",
    date: "October 2024",
    readMinutes: 11,
    excerpt: "Here is a fact that should change how you think about your marketing budget: in most Kenyan industries, competition for top Google rankings is startlingly low. While businesses in the US…",
    ctaTitle: "Ready to own your category on Google?",
    ctaBody: "We build SEO and content systems that keep generating leads long after the work is done.",
    image: "/brand/blog/seo-for-african-businesses.jpg",
    body: `<p>Here is a fact that should change how you think about your marketing budget: in most Kenyan industries, competition for top Google rankings is startlingly low. While businesses in the US and UK are spending millions fighting for positions on fiercely contested search terms, the same terms in Kenyan context often have three or four weak competitors in the top ten results — sites that could be outranked with a focused six-month content investment.</p><h2>Why African Businesses Underinvest in SEO</h2><p>The reasons are understandable: SEO is slow. The results take months to materialise. Paid advertising gives immediate, measurable returns. And most marketing advisors trained on Western playbooks prioritise channels with faster feedback loops. But this short-term thinking is leaving a long-term asset unclaimed.</p><h2>The Three Types of SEO That Matter</h2><ul><li><strong>Local SEO:</strong> Optimising your Google Business Profile, building local citations, and targeting location-specific keywords. If you have a physical location or serve a specific geographic area, this should be your first priority. Competition for "best [service] in Nairobi" terms is still low in most categories.</li><li><strong>Content SEO:</strong> Creating high-quality content that answers the questions your target customers are searching for. This builds topical authority over time — the signal Google uses to determine which sites deserve to rank for competitive terms.</li><li><strong>Technical SEO:</strong> Ensuring your website loads quickly, works on mobile, and is structured so Google can understand and index your content. Technical issues can cancel out even excellent content work.</li></ul><blockquote><p>Paid advertising stops the moment you stop paying. SEO compounds. A page that ranks today will often still be generating leads two years from now without additional investment.</p></blockquote><h2>Where to Start</h2><p>Audit your current search presence: what terms are you currently ranking for, how much organic traffic are you getting, and what are the highest-value terms in your category that you're not yet ranking for? This audit will tell you exactly where the opportunity is. Most businesses are surprised by how much ground they can gain with a focused, systematic approach to content and local SEO — without a single shilling of paid advertising.</p>`,
